WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.063
Policies target populations, never
people, and the populations are personas.

2
00:00:05.063 --> 00:00:07.827
They're defined in the
Federal Modernization

3
00:00:07.827 --> 00:00:10.374
Workbook's Book 02 and
kept identical here.

4
00:00:10.374 --> 00:00:14.068
Admins are users eligible for
or holding privileged roles,

5
00:00:14.068 --> 00:00:18.063
from the account-type attribute
or the PIM-eligible population.

6
00:00:18.063 --> 00:00:23.665
They carry the highest risk and get the
strictest policies: phishing-resistant MFA,

7
00:00:23.665 --> 00:00:28.212
a compliant device, trusted networks,
and privileged workstations.

8
00:00:28.212 --> 00:00:31.971
Internals are employees and
contractors with standard access,

9
00:00:31.971 --> 00:00:34.949
built dynamically from
the worker-type attribute.

10
00:00:34.949 --> 00:00:38.833
Externals are B2B guests
from partner organizations;

11
00:00:38.833 --> 00:00:42.463
Guests are ad hoc external
users from anywhere else.

12
00:00:42.463 --> 00:00:45.837
Workload identities are
single-tenant service principals.

13
00:00:45.837 --> 00:00:48.755
They must be assigned
to a policy directly,

14
00:00:48.755 --> 00:00:53.673
because a policy assigned to a group
that contains a service principal

15
00:00:53.673 --> 00:00:58.425
isn't enforced for it, and only two
policies reach them, CA015 and CA037.

16
00:00:58.425 --> 00:01:04.358
And break-glass: the two emergency accounts,
excluded from every enforced policy,

17
00:01:04.358 --> 00:01:07.900
and still recorded by CA090,
which is report-only.

18
00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:11.741
The library adds named
sub-populations inside the personas:

19
00:01:11.741 --> 00:01:16.273
web-only users without a managed
device, shared-device frontline staff,

20
00:01:16.273 --> 00:01:20.575
server administrators, Azure
operators and production operators.

21
00:01:20.575 --> 00:01:22.000
Each is a governed group.

22
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.900
Now the failure mode that drives one of the
library's most important design choices.

23
00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:30.288
Persona groups are dynamic,
built from attributes.

24
00:01:30.288 --> 00:01:34.262
A user whose attribute is empty
falls outside every persona group,

25
00:01:34.262 --> 00:01:38.237
and therefore outside every policy
that includes only that group.

26
00:01:38.237 --> 00:01:40.512
That's an exclusion nobody approved.

27
00:01:40.512 --> 00:01:46.534
So the library includes all users and
excludes narrowly, and it adds CA018,

28
00:01:46.534 --> 00:01:51.469
deliberately redundant: MFA for
all users and all resources,

29
00:01:51.469 --> 00:01:53.838
with no resource exclusions.

30
00:01:53.838 --> 00:01:57.852
It uses the plain require-MFA
control rather than a strength,

31
00:01:57.852 --> 00:02:02.640
so guests who sign in with email
one-time passcodes can still satisfy it.
